```java
import java.util.Arrays;

public class StringSwapper {

    /**
     * Swaps the contents of two character arrays in-place up to the length of the shorter array,
     * then rotates the elements of each array to maintain the original ordering of the excess elements.
     * 
     * @param arr1 The first character array.
     * @param arr2 The second character array.
     */
    public static void swapAndRotate(char[] arr1, char[] arr2) {
        int len1 = arr1.length;
        int len2 = arr2.length;
        int minLen = Math.min(len1, len2);

        // Swap elements up to the length of the shorter array
        for (int i = 0; i < minLen; i++) {
            arr1[i] = (char) (arr1[i] ^ arr2[i]);
            arr2[i] = (char) (arr1[i] ^ arr2[i]);
            arr1[i] = (char) (arr1[i] ^ arr2[i]);
        }

        // Rotate the excess elements of the longer array to maintain original order
        if (len1 > len2) {
            reverse(arr1, minLen, len1 - 1);
        } else if (len2 > len1) {
            reverse(arr2, minLen, len2 - 1);
        }

        System.out.println("After swapping and rotating: arr1 = " + Arrays.toString(arr1) + ", arr2 = " + Arrays.toString(arr2));
    }

    /**
     * Reverses a portion of a character array from the start index to the end index.
     * 
     * @param arr The character array.
     * @param start The start index of the portion to reverse.
     * @param end The end index of the portion to reverse.
     */
    private static void reverse(char[] arr, int start, int end) {
        while (start < end) {
            arr[start] = (char) (arr[start] ^ arr[end]);
            arr[end] = (char) (arr[start] ^ arr[end]);
            arr[start] = (char) (arr[start] ^ arr[end]);
            start++;
            end--;
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        char[] arr1 = "hello".toCharArray();
        char[] arr2 = "world".toCharArray();
        swapAndRotate(arr1, arr2);
    }
}
```

This Java program defines a method `swapAndRotate` that takes two character arrays as input and performs the specified operation. It first swaps the elements of the arrays up to the length of the shorter array using XOR operations to avoid the use of a temporary variable. Then, it rotates the excess elements of the longer array to maintain the original ordering. The `reverse` method is used to perform the rotation. Finally, the modified arrays are printed to the console.

To find the current timestamp from a given array of Unix timestamps, you can compare each timestamp in the array to the current system time. However, since Unix timestamps are in seconds and JavaScript's `Date.now()` returns the current time in milliseconds, you'll need to convert the current time to seconds by dividing by 1000.

Here's a JavaScript function that takes an array of Unix timestamps and returns the current timestamp:

```javascript
function getCurrentTimestamp(timestamps) {
  // Get the current time in milliseconds
  const now = Date.now();
  // Convert the current time to seconds
  const nowInSeconds = Math.floor(now / 1000);

  // Find the timestamp that matches the current time
  const currentTimestamp = timestamps.find(timestamp => timestamp === nowInSeconds);

  // Return the current timestamp if found, otherwise return null
  return currentTimestamp !== undefined ? currentTimestamp : null;
}

// Example usage:
const timestamps = [1679272800, 1679276400, 1679280000, Date.now() / 1000];
const currentTimestamp = getCurrentTimestamp(timestamps);
console.log(currentTimestamp);
```

Please note that the exact match might not always be possible due to the precision of the Unix timestamp and the current time. If you need to account for a small time difference, you can adjust the comparison in the `find` method to allow for a certain margin of error.

Also, keep in mind that the `Date.now()` function returns the current time in the system's local timezone, so if you are dealing with timestamps in a different timezone, you might need to adjust the time accordingly.

