`std::visit` is a function in the C++17 standard library that allows you to perform operations on a `std::variant` based on its current type. Here's how you can use it:

Let's say you have a `std::variant` that can hold either an `int` or a `std::string`:

```cpp
#include <variant>
#include <string>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    std::variant<int, std::string> v;

    // Set the variant to hold an int
    v = 42;

    // Now use std::visit to print the value, if it's an int
    std::visit([](auto&& arg) {
        std::cout << "Variant holds: " << arg << '\n';
    }, v);

    // Set the variant to hold a string
    v = std::string("Hello, World!");

    // Now use std::visit to print the value, if it's a string
    std::visit([](auto&& arg) {
        std::cout << "Variant holds: " << arg << '\n';
    }, v);

    return 0;
}
```

However, this will not compile because the lambda function does not know how to handle both types. To fix this, you need to provide an overloaded function for each possible type in the variant:

```cpp
#include <variant>
#include <string>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    std::variant<int, std::string> v;

    // Set the variant to hold an int
    v = 42;

    // Now use std::visit to print the value, if it's an int
    std::visit([](int i) {
        std::cout << "Variant holds: " << i << '\n';
    }, v);

    // Set the variant to hold a string
    v = std::string("Hello, World!");

    // Now use std::visit to print the value, if it's a string
    std::visit([](const std::string& s) {
        std::cout << "Variant holds: " << s << '\n';
    }, v);

    return 0;
}
```

Alternatively, you can use a template to handle multiple types:

```cpp
