One approach to solve this problem is to iterate through the string character by character and keep track of whether the current character is a digit or not. If the current character is a digit, we add it to a temporary string variable that holds the current number we are extracting. If the current character is not a digit, we check if we have already started extracting a number (i.e., if the temporary string variable is not empty). If we have, then we know that we have finished extracting a number, so we add it to a vector of numbers and reset the temporary string variable. We continue iterating until we have processed the entire string.

Here is an implementation of this approach:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <string>

int main() {
    std::string input = "Thor 45plenty!9stars7";
    std::vector<std::string> numbers;

    std::string currentNumber = "";
    bool isDigit = false;
    for (char c : input) {
        if (isdigit(c)) {
            currentNumber += c;
            isDigit = true;
        } else {
            if (isDigit) {
                numbers.push_back(currentNumber);
                currentNumber = "";
                isDigit = false;
            }
        }
    }

    // Add the last number if there is one
    if (isDigit) {
        numbers.push_back(currentNumber);
    }

    // Print the extracted numbers
    for (std::string number : numbers) {
        std::cout << number << std::endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

